WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.199 --> 00:00:04.350
Not many people remember the original Spider-Man
movies — and we're not talking about the

00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:05.829
Tobey Maguire films.

00:00:05.829 --> 00:00:11.360
This forgotten trilogy from the 1970s played
in theaters worldwide, marking a curious debut

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:13.429
for the webslinger on the silver screen.

00:00:13.429 --> 00:00:17.850
Let's break down the original live-action
Spider-Man movies you probably never saw.

00:00:18.850 --> 00:00:21.980
These three Spider-Man movies weren't originally
supposed to be movies.

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:26.680
In fact, all three hail from the live-action
TV show The Amazing Spider-Man that aired

00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:27.680
on CBS.

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:31.900
Part of a wave of live-action superhero TV
shows in the late ‘70s that delivered the

00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:37.960
likes of The Incredible Hulk and Wonder Woman,
The Amazing Spider-Man aired from 1977 to

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:39.000
1979.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:44.170
The feature-length pilot that kicked off this
trilogy also served as a movie entitled Spider-Man,

00:00:44.170 --> 00:00:46.680
the first entry in this trilogy of Spider-Man
movies.

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:51.220
The subsequent two installments of the trilogy,
Spider-Man Strikes Back and Spider-Man: The

00:00:51.220 --> 00:00:55.760
Dragon's Challenge, were each comprised of
two other episodes of the Amazing Spider-Man

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:56.760
show.

00:00:56.760 --> 00:01:01.000
Headlining all three movies is Nicholas Hammond
as Peter Parker, while Robert F. Simon portrays

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:04.000
J. Jonah Jameson in each entry of the trilogy.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:07.579
Beyond them, each movie is primarily stocked
up with a whole new cast.

00:01:07.579 --> 00:01:11.719
Whereas modern Spider-Man movies obviously
get massive global theatrical releases, the

00:01:11.719 --> 00:01:15.590
original Spider-Man trilogy had a much more
limited theatrical reach.

00:01:15.590 --> 00:01:20.130
Domestically, audiences only saw these episodes
as part of Spider-Man's brief stint on the

00:01:20.130 --> 00:01:21.130
airwaves.

00:01:21.130 --> 00:01:24.939
In certain international territories, though,
these episodes were repackaged as a trilogy

00:01:24.939 --> 00:01:29.440
of movies distributed by Columbia Pictures,
who would go on to be the distributor of the

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:32.219
modern Spider-Man movies in the 21st century.

00:01:33.219 --> 00:01:37.469
Peter Parker's debut live-action movie follows
a plot familiar to anyone who's seen Tobey

00:01:37.469 --> 00:01:40.979
Maguire and Andrew Garfield's first takes
on Spider-Man.

00:01:40.979 --> 00:01:45.929
Nicholas Hammond's debut as Spider-Man begins
with the superhero's alter-ego, Peter Parker,

00:01:45.929 --> 00:01:48.249
getting bitten by a radioactive spider.

00:01:48.249 --> 00:01:52.539
Soon after, he discovers that he's been bestowed
with extraordinary superpowers like being

00:01:52.539 --> 00:01:54.069
able to crawl up walls.

00:01:54.069 --> 00:01:58.409
Future incarnations of this storyline would
see Parker facing off against Green Goblin

00:01:58.409 --> 00:01:59.409
and The Lizard.

00:01:59.409 --> 00:02:03.869
For this pilot, Spidey would go toe-to-toe
with Edward Byron, a guru who's hypnotizing

00:02:03.869 --> 00:02:05.499
New Yorkers to commit crimes.

00:02:05.499 --> 00:02:09.860
Clumsy fight scenes involving Byron's henchmen
and an assortment of near-death experiences

00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:14.200
involving hypnosis ensue as Spider-Man does
his best to stop Byron.

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:19.360
Unlike the other two entries in this series,
the 1977 Spider-Man was always intended to

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:20.909
be a feature-length story.

00:02:20.909 --> 00:02:25.069
In this era, it was a common practice for
TV pilots to run this long, as was giving

00:02:25.069 --> 00:02:27.890
these projects international theatrical releases.

00:02:27.890 --> 00:02:31.590
Going down this route allowed the producers
of pilots like Spider-Man to have their cake

00:02:31.590 --> 00:02:35.950
and eat it too, hedging their bets on both
the domestic TV market and the international

00:02:35.950 --> 00:02:37.200
theatrical circuit.

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:41.849
Official box office figures for Spider-Man
'77 have never been released, but the book

00:02:41.849 --> 00:02:45.920
Age of TV Heroes: The Live-Action Adventures
of Your Favorite Comic Book Characters by

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:51.510
Jason Hofius and George Khoury claims the
production earned $9 million in overseas grosses.

00:02:52.610 --> 00:02:57.490
Whereas the 1977 Spider-Man movie dealt with
villainy related to hypnosis, the sequel,

00:02:57.490 --> 00:02:59.969
Spider-Man Strikes Back, is all about nuclear
warfare.

00:02:59.969 --> 00:03:04.370
The topic is explored through Spider-Man's
confrontation with a villain by the name of

00:03:04.370 --> 00:03:07.120
Mr. White — no, not that Mr. White.

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:08.900
And no, not that one either.

00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:13.159
Spidey's newest foe hatches a devious plan
that involves bombing the World Trade Center.

00:03:13.159 --> 00:03:18.780
Much like the infamous finale of the 1976
King Kong, also set on the Twin Towers, Mr.

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:23.689
White's evil plan makes watching Spider-Man
Strikes Back in 2020 a far more uncomfortable

00:03:23.689 --> 00:03:27.510
experience than one would expect from a live-action
Spider-Man movie.

00:03:27.510 --> 00:03:30.540
Thankfully, this isn't the only scheme Mr.
White has concocted.

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:35.150
Because this is two standalone episodes stitched
together into a movie, Mr. White eventually

00:03:35.150 --> 00:03:40.040
moves on from nuclear weapons to an assassination
attempt on the President of the United States.

00:03:40.040 --> 00:03:44.560
"I think I may have to try a new approach
with this piece of merchandise."

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:49.900
The film eventually ends with Mr. White escaping
and promising to get his revenge on Spider-Man.

00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:53.959
Given that Mr. White never appeared again,
this was a promise he couldn't keep, which,

00:03:53.959 --> 00:03:56.500
as Spider-Man knows, is the best kind of promise.

00:03:56.500 --> 00:04:00.950
Whereas the previous Spider-Man movie got
a general theatrical release internationally,

00:04:00.950 --> 00:04:04.450
Spider-Man Strikes Back was given a far more
restrained theatrical launch.

00:04:04.450 --> 00:04:08.640
Only a handful of European territories, including
Germany, screened the title.

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:13.499
Its overseas theatrical presence was so minimal
that there are no recorded box office figures

00:04:13.499 --> 00:04:14.659
for the project.

00:04:14.659 --> 00:04:19.200
Much like Mr. White leaving the scene of a
dastardly crime, Spider-Man Strikes Back vanished

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:21.080
from theaters without a trace.

00:04:21.080 --> 00:04:25.880
"The one thing they love more than a hero
is to see a hero fail."

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:30.890
Wrapping up the trilogy was Spider-Man: The
Dragon's Challenge, which saw the web-crawler

00:04:30.890 --> 00:04:35.290
facing off against a villain named Zeider,
a wealthy man who wants to kill an old colleague

00:04:35.290 --> 00:04:38.540
of J. Jonah Jameson by the name of Min Lo
Chan.

00:04:38.540 --> 00:04:43.260
With incriminating information on Zeider in
his possession, Min Lo Chan's life is in constant

00:04:43.260 --> 00:04:44.260
danger.

00:04:44.260 --> 00:04:47.850
The only person who could possibly protect
Lo Chan and his niece Emily from these threats

00:04:47.850 --> 00:04:48.850
is Spider-Man.

00:04:48.850 --> 00:04:52.980
Thus, the web-crawler ends up protecting Min
from the various bruisers Zeider sends to

00:04:52.980 --> 00:04:54.200
assassinate him.

00:04:54.200 --> 00:04:59.200
Eventually, Peter Parker uses his wits to
expose Zeider and his treachery, thus preserving

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:00.820
the life of Min Lo Chan.

00:05:00.820 --> 00:05:04.980
Like Spider-Man Strikes Back, The Dragon's
Challenge received a minor theatrical release

00:05:04.980 --> 00:05:10.730
in international territories, with this particular
entry screening in countries such as Australia.

00:05:10.730 --> 00:05:15.280
Also like its predecessor, The Dragon's Challenge
has never reported box office figures for

00:05:15.280 --> 00:05:16.650
its theatrical run.

00:05:16.650 --> 00:05:20.810
This means that the largest legacy of this
project is two members of its supporting cast.

00:05:20.810 --> 00:05:24.560
Firstly, there was Rosalind Chao, who played
Emily in the movie.

00:05:24.560 --> 00:05:29.160
She scored an early acting credit here before
going on to appear in numerous TV shows and

00:05:29.160 --> 00:05:31.680
movies, including Star Trek: Deep Space Nine.

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:36.100
She's also reportedly part of the cast of
the upcoming Marvel Studios movie Shang-Chi

00:05:36.100 --> 00:05:38.120
and the Legend of the Ten Rings.

00:05:38.120 --> 00:05:41.730
Also appearing in Spider-Man: The Dragon's
Challenge, in the role of Major Collins, was

00:05:41.730 --> 00:05:42.910
Ted Danson.

00:05:42.910 --> 00:05:48.130
When the episodes comprising this movie originally
aired in 1978, Collins served as Danson's

00:05:48.130 --> 00:05:50.060
third credited appearance as an actor.

00:05:51.060 --> 00:05:55.200
In many movie trilogies, it's easy to discern
at least some kind of overarching story.

00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:59.300
Some of those three-movie arcs can be loose,
like the Sam Raimi Spider-Man trilogy and

00:05:59.300 --> 00:06:03.880
its payoffs relating to Sandman, Uncle Ben,
Harry Obsorn, and the Green Goblin.

00:06:03.880 --> 00:06:07.930
Other trilogies, like The Lord of the Rings,
are so tightly wound together that it's more-or-less

00:06:07.930 --> 00:06:11.110
not possible to comfortably jump in at the
middle of the story.

00:06:11.110 --> 00:06:15.211
That's not the case with this inaugural Spider-Man
trilogy, for the simple reason that they were

00:06:15.211 --> 00:06:18.400
never meant to be an interconnected series
of films to begin with.

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:23.840
There's a sense of disconnect across the three
movies that makes it unique among movie trilogies,

00:06:23.840 --> 00:06:25.690
particularly ones starring superheroes.

00:06:25.690 --> 00:06:31.100
The original 1977 Spider-Man is a standalone
feature-length pilot meant to introduce viewers

00:06:31.100 --> 00:06:36.080
to the idea of weekly Spider-Man adventures
rather than set up feature-length sequels.

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:40.340
As for the two follow-ups, they're each just
multiple episodes of that TV show that have

00:06:40.340 --> 00:06:42.010
been roughly jammed together.

00:06:42.010 --> 00:06:46.110
This explains why Spider-Man Strikes Back,
for example, stops and starts its plot for

00:06:46.110 --> 00:06:49.080
two wildly different evil plans from Mr. White.

00:06:49.080 --> 00:06:53.570
Each plan was originally the center of its
own TV episode rather than two halves of a

00:06:53.570 --> 00:06:54.570
movie.

00:06:54.570 --> 00:06:59.280
This results in very little storytelling cohesion
across these Spider-Man features as individual

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:02.420
properties, let alone across the ramshackle
trilogy as a whole.

00:07:03.420 --> 00:07:07.840
With the exception of J. Jonah Jameson, Spidey's
supporting cast in these movies is almost

00:07:07.840 --> 00:07:12.640
entirely composed of new characters who have
nothing to do with the Spider-Man comics.

00:07:12.640 --> 00:07:16.190
"Who is that character anyway?"

00:07:16.190 --> 00:07:20.800
For instance, in the first Spider-Man movie,
a character named Judy Tyler is the most prominent

00:07:20.800 --> 00:07:25.470
woman in Peter Parker's life instead of Gwen
Stacy or Mary Jane Watson.

00:07:25.470 --> 00:07:29.660
Spider-Man's best friend Harry Osborn is also
absent from all three features.

00:07:29.660 --> 00:07:34.590
The presence of new characters is especially
notable when it comes to the trilogy's villains.

00:07:34.590 --> 00:07:39.370
Instead of facing off against seminal foes
like Green Goblin or Kraven the Hunter, international

00:07:39.370 --> 00:07:44.400
moviegoers were treated to the sight of Spidey
waging war against Edward Byron and Mr. White.

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:48.670
None of the oversized figures in colorful
costumes that usually pick a fight with Spider-Man

00:07:48.670 --> 00:07:49.890
would be found here.

00:07:49.890 --> 00:07:54.130
The lack of Spidey's iconic supporting cast
and foes from the comics explain a lot as

00:07:54.130 --> 00:07:57.180
to why these movies didn't win over comic
book devotees.

00:07:57.180 --> 00:08:01.520
They also reinforce how these three movies
are such strange outliers in Spider-Man's

00:08:01.520 --> 00:08:02.560
feature film career.

00:08:03.560 --> 00:08:08.300
Opinions differ about the quality of the 21st
century Spider-Man movies, but one thing is

00:08:08.300 --> 00:08:12.330
clear: all of them garnered better marks than
the original three Spider-Man movies.

00:08:12.330 --> 00:08:18.660
A retrospective review of the 1977 Spider-Man
movie from JoBlo described the project as

00:08:18.660 --> 00:08:23.450
goofy, cheesy, and penny-pinching -- a critique
that can be applied to every movie in the

00:08:23.450 --> 00:08:24.450
series.

00:08:24.450 --> 00:08:29.050
Criticism of the low budgets are common; though
presented theatrically overseas, the TV origins

00:08:29.050 --> 00:08:32.380
of these Spider-Man movies was readily apparent
on the screen.

00:08:32.380 --> 00:08:34.620
Reviews for the follow-ups didn't get any
better.

00:08:34.620 --> 00:08:38.690
Spider-Man Strikes Back was reviled by The
Dissolve for many reasons, with one observation

00:08:38.690 --> 00:08:39.860
being that

00:08:39.860 --> 00:08:43.010
"Spider-Man doesn't throw his first punch
until the film is half over."

00:08:43.010 --> 00:08:47.070
Meanwhile, the conclusion of the trilogy,
Spider-Man: The Dragon's Challenge, was declared

00:08:47.070 --> 00:08:51.390
by Inverse to be the worst Spider-Man movie
ever made" due to the film's overwhelmingly

00:08:51.390 --> 00:08:52.800
tedious nature.

00:08:52.800 --> 00:08:57.030
While each of the individual films drew their
own unique criticisms, the critical reception

00:08:57.030 --> 00:09:01.120
to the first trilogy of Spider-Man movies
was subpar across the board.

00:09:01.120 --> 00:09:05.300
Worst of all, though, was that Spider-Man
co-creator Stan Lee was critical of the TV

00:09:05.300 --> 00:09:07.230
show that spawned these three movies.

00:09:07.230 --> 00:09:11.730
Echoing modern complaints about the lack of
personality in this original Spider-Man trilogy,

00:09:11.730 --> 00:09:13.100
Lee once said of the show,

00:09:13.100 --> 00:09:14.340
"They left out the humor.

00:09:14.340 --> 00:09:19.110
They left out the human interest and personality
and playing up characterizations and personal

00:09:19.110 --> 00:09:20.110
problems."

00:09:21.110 --> 00:09:24.760
Usually, anything with the Spider-Man logo
is guaranteed to get some form of release.

00:09:24.760 --> 00:09:28.460
But there are limits to everything, and even
the lucrative nature of the character hasn't

00:09:28.460 --> 00:09:31.310
guaranteed new home video releases for these
three movies.

00:09:31.310 --> 00:09:37.140
Though put out on VHS in the 1980s, the original
live-action Spider-Man trilogy hasn't found

00:09:37.140 --> 00:09:40.070
its way into any other home media format since.

00:09:40.070 --> 00:09:44.610
This mirrors the scarcity of the Amazing Spider-Man
TV show as a whole, which has been entirely

00:09:44.610 --> 00:09:49.150
denied a proper home video release save for
a handful of episodes making their way onto

00:09:49.150 --> 00:09:50.150
VHS.

00:09:50.150 --> 00:09:54.930
By contrast, the Spider-Man show from Japan,
released in the same era as The Amazing Spider-Man,

00:09:54.930 --> 00:09:58.810
has managed to get released on physical home
media in Region 2 markets.

00:09:58.810 --> 00:10:02.760
The age of streaming has offered no salvation
for the increased availability of these original

00:10:02.760 --> 00:10:04.210
three Spider-Man movies.

00:10:04.210 --> 00:10:07.779
They have never appeared on any streaming
platform and no plans have been announced

00:10:07.779 --> 00:10:12.690
to have them stream alongside other Spider-Man
programming on a platform like Disney+.

00:10:12.690 --> 00:10:17.080
The negative reception associated with the
program, as well as a lack of a major fanbase

00:10:17.080 --> 00:10:21.370
to champion for its release, has led to these
three Spider-Man movies gathering dust on

00:10:21.370 --> 00:10:23.730
a shelf somewhere in a corporate vault.

00:10:24.730 --> 00:10:28.530
While comic books are the go-to source of
creative inspiration for adaptations of Marvel

00:10:28.530 --> 00:10:32.780
Comics characters, certain TV shows, like
The Incredible Hulk, have managed to leave

00:10:32.780 --> 00:10:34.870
a lasting creative legacy.

00:10:34.870 --> 00:10:37.820
Such was not the case with this trilogy of
Spider-Man movies.

00:10:37.820 --> 00:10:42.010
Much like the TV show they spun off from,
these Spider-Man films have mostly vanished

00:10:42.010 --> 00:10:43.529
from the pop culture consciousness.

00:10:43.529 --> 00:10:46.060
"Do us both a favor — forget it."

00:10:46.060 --> 00:10:50.050
Part of that is due to the trilogy's reliance
on a down-to-Earth vision of Spider-Man.

00:10:50.050 --> 00:10:54.140
The lack of exciting or strange elements in
these films has kept them from turning into

00:10:54.140 --> 00:10:57.660
a cult hit like the 1970s Japanese Spider-Man
show.

00:10:57.660 --> 00:11:01.760
The lack of a proper home video or streaming
release has similarly ensured that the first

00:11:01.760 --> 00:11:05.080
three Spider-Man movies have become a pop
culture footnote.

00:11:05.080 --> 00:11:08.900
In other ways, the property's longevity has
been hurt by how it hasn't been referenced

00:11:08.900 --> 00:11:14.450
in other Spider-Man media, rarely being treated
as a "real" adaptation of the Spider-Man story.

00:11:14.450 --> 00:11:18.890
One exception to this phenomenon came in the
2019 edition of Entertainment Weekly's "The

00:11:18.890 --> 00:11:23.380
Ultimate Guide to The Avengers," which recognized
Nicholas Hammond as one of the four actors

00:11:23.380 --> 00:11:25.560
to have portrayed Spider-Man in live action.

00:11:25.560 --> 00:11:29.850
It was a rare moment of recognition for the
actor that first took the web-crawler to the

00:11:29.850 --> 00:11:30.850
movies.

00:11:30.850 --> 00:11:32.790
Check out one of our newest videos right here!

00:11:32.790 --> 00:11:37.400
Plus, even more Looper videos about Spider-Man
and his amazing friends are coming soon.

